                0 to "The scenario says nothing about the order of the items; the gap is that the goal is not communicated.",
                2 to "Accepting the Increment is not in the Guide at all.",
                3 to "The Sprint Goal is defined by the whole Scrum Team at Sprint Planning; it is not the Product Owner's to write alone.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-29", "psm-product-owner", "What is the main concern?", listOf("The Scrum Master should have been the one keeping the spreadsheet", "None, as long as the Product Owner tells the Developers the next items at Sprint Planning", "The Product Backlog is not transparent, visible and understood, which the Product Owner is accountable for", "The Developers should reorder the shared backlog themselves to match their own view"), setOf(2),
            "Effective Product Backlog management includes ensuring the Product Backlog is transparent, visible and understood. The Guide also says the Product Owner's decisions are visible in the content and ordering of the Product Backlog, which a private spreadsheet hides.",
            scenario = "The shared Product Backlog has not been updated for weeks. The Product Owner keeps the real order of the work in a private spreadsheet that only she sees.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting because the Scrum Master helps with backlog techniques, but keeping the backlog visible is the Product Owner's accountability, and a private copy is the problem whoever keeps it.",
                1 to "Revealing items only at Sprint Planning leaves the team and stakeholders unable to inspect the plan, and gets in the way of refinement in between.",
                3 to "Ordering is the Product Owner's accountability; the Developers would be taking it over, not fixing transparency.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-30", "psm-product-owner", "What does the Scrum Guide say?", listOf("It sets no ordering criteria; ordering the Product Backlog is the Product Owner's decision", "The stakeholder is right: the Guide requires ordering by business value", "Technical items do not belong in the Product Backlog at all", "The Developers order the technical items, and the Product Owner orders the rest"), setOf(0),
            "The Guide makes the Product Owner accountable for ordering Product Backlog items and does not prescribe how. The Product Backlog is the single source of work for the Scrum Team, so technical work belongs in it too.",
            scenario = "The Product Owner places a technical upgrade above several popular features, after the Developers explained that it greatly reduces risk. A stakeholder says the Product Backlog must be ordered by business value alone.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                1 to "Tempting because value is central to the Product Owner's role, but the Guide gives no rule that the order must be by business value alone.",
                2 to "The Product Backlog is the single source of work undertaken by the Scrum Team, and that includes technical work.",
                3 to "The Developers can advise, but ordering the whole Product Backlog is the Product Owner's accountability.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-31", "psm-product-owner", "Who does the Scrum Guide say takes part in the Sprint Retrospective?", listOf("The Developers only, with the Scrum Master facilitating", "The Developers and the Scrum Master only", "The Scrum Team, which includes the Product Owner", "Whoever the Developers choose to invite"), setOf(2),
            "The Guide says the Scrum Team inspects how the last Sprint went at the Sprint Retrospective. The Scrum Team is the Product Owner, the Scrum Master and the Developers, so the Product Owner takes part.",
            scenario = "The Developers tell the Product Owner she is not welcome at the Sprint Retrospective, because \"it is our meeting\".",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting if the Retrospective is confused with the Daily Scrum, which is for the Developers. The Retrospective is for the whole Scrum Team.",
                1 to "Leaving out the Product Owner leaves out a member of the Scrum Team, whose interactions with the others are part of what is inspected.",
                3 to "The Retrospective is not by invitation; it is an event for the whole Scrum Team.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-32", "psm-product-owner", "Who ensures that attendees are prepared to discuss the most important Product Backlog items and how they map to the Product Goal?", listOf("The Scrum Master", "The Product Owner", "The Developers", "The key stakeholders"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide says the Product Owner ensures that attendees are prepared to discuss the most important Product Backlog items and how they map to the Product Goal. Refinement before Sprint Planning is how items usually become clear enough.",
            scenario = "At Sprint Planning, the items at the top of the Product Backlog are vague, and the team spends hours working out what they mean.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting because the Scrum Master helps the team understand the need for clear items, but the Guide names the Product Owner for this preparation.",
                2 to "The Developers take part in refinement and sizing, but the Guide places this preparation with the Product Owner.",
                3 to "Stakeholders may be invited to give advice, but they are not accountable for preparing Sprint Planning.",
            ),
        ),

        // Scrum Master
        B.q("psm-p2-33", "psm-scrum-master", "Which part of the Scrum Master's service to the Scrum Team applies most directly?", listOf("Ensuring that all Scrum events take place and are positive, productive, and kept within the timebox", "Helping find techniques for effective Product Backlog management", "Planning and advising Scrum implementations within the organisation", "Removing barriers between stakeholders and Scrum Teams"), setOf(0),
            "One way the Guide says the Scrum Master serves the Scrum Team is by ensuring that all Scrum events take place and are positive, productive, and kept within the timebox. A Retrospective where people are afraid to speak is neither positive nor productive.",
            scenario = "Sprint Retrospectives have turned into blame sessions. Two Developers attack each other's work, and the rest now stay silent until the event ends.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                1 to "That is a way of serving the Product Owner; the problem here is how the event itself is running.",
                2 to "That is a way of serving the organisation; it does not address a Retrospective that has become hostile.",
                3 to "That is about stakeholders and the team; the problem here is inside the Scrum Team's own event.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-34", "psm-scrum-master", "Which way of serving the Product Owner, described in the Guide, fits best?", listOf("Taking over the plan so the Product Owner can focus on stakeholders", "Helping establish empirical product planning for a complex environment", "Telling the Developers to commit to the plan's dates", "Ordering the Product Backlog to match the plan"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide lists helping establish empirical product planning for a complex environment among the ways the Scrum Master serves the Product Owner. The aim is planning that adapts to what is learned, not a better fixed plan.",
            scenario = "The Product Owner's detailed annual plans keep falling apart within weeks. She asks the Scrum Master for help.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The Scrum Master helps; taking over product planning would move the Product Owner's accountability to the Scrum Master.",
                2 to "Pressing for fixed dates is the opposite of empirical planning, and the Developers' commitment is the Sprint Goal.",
                3 to "Ordering the Product Backlog is the Product Owner's accountability.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-35", "psm-scrum-master", "Is this part of how the Scrum Master serves the Product Owner?", listOf("No: stakeholders are the Product Owner's business alone, and the Scrum Master should stay out", "No: the Scrum Master serves only the Developers", "Yes, and the Scrum Master should then set the priorities, since he is neutral", "Yes: the Guide includes facilitating stakeholder collaboration as requested or needed"), setOf(3),
            "Facilitating stakeholder collaboration as requested or needed is one of the ways the Guide says the Scrum Master serves the Product Owner. The Product Owner still makes the ordering decisions.",
            scenario = "The Product Owner asks the Scrum Master to run a workshop where three competing stakeholder groups work out the next priorities with her.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting, but the Guide lists stakeholder facilitation among the Scrum Master's services to the Product Owner.",
                1 to "The Guide says the Scrum Master serves the Scrum Team, the Product Owner and the organisation.",
                2 to "Facilitating is not deciding; ordering the Product Backlog stays with the Product Owner.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-36", "psm-scrum-master", "What does the Guide say about the Scrum Master and impediments?", listOf("The Scrum Master causes the removal of impediments, which can include coaching the team to remove those within its own control", "The Scrum Master must personally remove every impediment the team reports", "Impediments are the Product Owner's to remove, since they affect value", "Impediments are raised and dealt with only at the Sprint Retrospective"), setOf(0),
            "The Guide says the Scrum Master serves the team by causing the removal of impediments to its progress, and by coaching the members in self-management. A self-managing team deals with what it can; the Scrum Master makes sure the rest gets removed.",
            scenario = "The team's test environment is slow. The Developers say fixing it is the Scrum Master's job, and do nothing about it until he does.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                1 to "Tempting, but the Guide's words are causing the removal; the Scrum Master need not do it personally, and doing everything for the team weakens self-management.",
                2 to "The Guide places causing the removal of impediments with the Scrum Master, not the Product Owner.",
                3 to "Waiting for the Retrospective delays adaptation; impediments are identified at the Daily Scrum and can be handled whenever they appear.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-37", "psm-scrum-master", "What should the Scrum Master do?", listOf("Write the monthly report so the stakeholders still get information", "Accept it, since stakeholder relations are outside Scrum", "Work to remove this barrier, for example by helping the manager see why stakeholders need to take part in the Sprint Review", "Ask the Product Owner to be the only channel, and stop inviting stakeholders to the Sprint Review"), setOf(2),
            "One way the Scrum Master serves the organisation is by removing barriers between stakeholders and Scrum Teams. The Sprint Review relies on key stakeholders inspecting the outcome with the Scrum Team.",
            scenario = "A manager tells stakeholders they may not speak to the Scrum Team, and must wait for a monthly status report instead.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting because it keeps people informed, but a report replaces the collaboration the Guide expects and leaves the barrier in place.",
                1 to "The Guide makes removing barriers between stakeholders and Scrum Teams part of the Scrum Master's service to the organisation.",
                3 to "The Sprint Review is held with key stakeholders; shutting them out would harm inspection rather than help it.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-38", "psm-scrum-master", "What is the Scrum Master's best response?", listOf("Agree, and cover this Sprint's work in the next Sprint's Review", "Agree, as long as the Product Owner emails the stakeholders a summary", "Extend the Sprint by a few days so there is more to show", "Ensure the Sprint Review takes place, because it is where the outcome is inspected and next steps decided, whatever the size of the Increment"), setOf(3),
            "The Guide says the Scrum Master ensures all Scrum events take place and are positive, productive and kept within the timebox. The Sprint Review inspects the outcome of the Sprint and decides future adaptations, which matters most when a Sprint went badly.",
            scenario = "Only one small item was finished this Sprint. The Developers suggest skipping the Sprint Review because there is \"nothing worth showing\".",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Each Sprint has its own Sprint Review; skipping one loses a chance to inspect and adapt.",
                1 to "An email is not the working session with stakeholders that the Guide describes.",
                2 to "A Sprint has a fixed length; it is not extended to make the Review look better.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-39", "psm-scrum-master", "Which are ways the Guide says the Scrum Master serves the organisation? (choose two)", listOf("Appointing the Product Owners for the new teams", "Planning and advising Scrum implementations within the organisation", "Assigning Developers to each new team", "Helping employees and stakeholders understand and enact an empirical approach for complex work", "Setting the Product Goal for each new team"), setOf(1, 3),
            "The Guide lists four ways: leading, training and coaching the organisation in its Scrum adoption; planning and advising Scrum implementations; helping employees and stakeholders understand and enact an empirical approach for complex work; and removing barriers between stakeholders and Scrum Teams.",
            scenario = "A director plans to start four new Scrum Teams and asks the Scrum Master what help he can give the organisation.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Appointing Product Owners is not among the Scrum Master's services in the Guide.",
                2 to "The Guide gives the Scrum Master no staffing authority; teams are structured and empowered by the organisation.",
                4 to "The Product Goal is developed and communicated by each team's Product Owner.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-40", "psm-scrum-master", "Which way of serving the Scrum Team, from the Guide, addresses this most directly?", listOf("Coaching the team to loosen the Definition of Done", "Assigning each Developer one item at a time so fewer are started", "Helping the Scrum Team focus on creating high-value Increments that meet the Definition of Done", "Facilitating stakeholder collaboration for the Product Owner"), setOf(2),
            "The Guide lists helping the Scrum Team focus on creating high-value Increments that meet the Definition of Done among the Scrum Master's services to the team. Work that does not meet the Definition of Done is not part of an Increment.",
            scenario = "The team starts many items each Sprint but finishes few. At each Sprint Review most of the work is \"nearly done\" and cannot be shown.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Lowering quality to look Done hides the problem; the Definition of Done describes the quality the Increment needs.",
                1 to "Tempting because it limits work in progress, but the Scrum Master does not assign work; the team decides who does what.",
                3 to "That is a service to the Product Owner, and it does not address unfinished work.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-41", "psm-scrum-master", "Which actions fit the Scrum Master's accountability in the Guide? (choose two)", listOf("Rewriting the items himself before every Sprint Planning so the Product Owner need not", "Helping the Scrum Team understand the need for clear and concise Product Backlog items", "Introducing a rule that items must be signed off by the Scrum Master", "Helping find techniques for effective Product Backlog management"), setOf(1, 3),
            "The Guide lists both as ways the Scrum Master serves the Product Owner: helping find techniques for effective Product Goal definition and Product Backlog management, and helping the Scrum Team understand the need for clear and concise Product Backlog items.",
            scenario = "The Developers keep misunderstanding Product Backlog items, and the Product Owner is unsure how to write them better.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting as a quick fix, but it is not among the Scrum Master's services in the Guide, and it leaves the Product Owner no better at work she is accountable for.",
                2 to "The Guide gives the Scrum Master no approval power over the Product Backlog; the organisation must respect the Product Owner's decisions.",
            ),
        ),

        // Traps
        B.q("psm-p2-42", "psm-traps", "What does the Scrum Guide say?", listOf("The consultant is right: only practices named in the Guide may be used", "The team must choose between Scrum and these practices", "These practices may be used only with the Scrum Master's approval", "Scrum is a framework within which various processes, techniques and methods can be employed"), setOf(3),
            "The Guide describes Scrum as purposefully incomplete and says various processes, techniques and methods can be employed within the framework. What it does not allow is changing or leaving out Scrum's own elements.",
            scenario = "A team uses a Kanban board, pair programming and test-driven development inside its Sprints. A consultant tells them that anything the Scrum Guide does not mention is forbidden in Scrum.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting because the Guide is strict about its own elements, but it expects other practices to be used within the framework.",
                1 to "Scrum wraps around existing practices or renders them unnecessary; there is no need to choose between them.",
                2 to "The Guide sets no approval step; the self-managing Scrum Team decides how it works.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-43", "psm-traps", "Which statement is accurate?", listOf("The Definition of Ready is the commitment for the Product Backlog", "The 2020 Guide added a Definition of Ready alongside the Definition of Done", "A Definition of Ready replaces Product Backlog refinement", "Scrum's artifacts are the Product Backlog, the Sprint Backlog and the Increment; a Definition of Ready is not part of Scrum, though a team may choose to use one"), setOf(3),
            "Scrum's artifacts are the Product Backlog, the Sprint Backlog and the Increment, with the Product Goal, the Sprint Goal and the Definition of Done as their commitments. The Guide speaks of items being ready for selection after refinement, but defines no Definition of Ready.",
            scenario = "A team adopts a Definition of Ready: no item may enter Sprint Planning without full acceptance criteria and a finished UI mock-up. A new member is told it is a required Scrum artifact.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The commitment for the Product Backlog is the Product Goal.",
                1 to "Tempting because the name sounds official, but the Guide has a Definition of Done only.",
                2 to "Refinement is in the Guide; a readiness checklist is at most a team practice on top of it.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-44", "psm-traps", "What fits the Scrum Guide?", listOf("The manager is right: the Sprint Review must last four hours for a one-month Sprint", "Four hours is a maximum, not a required length; the event can end once its purpose is met", "The time saved must be added to the Sprint Retrospective", "The Sprint Review should be repeated the next day to reach four hours"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide says the Sprint Review is timeboxed to a maximum of four hours for a one-month Sprint. A timebox limits how long an event can take; it is not a length the event must fill.",
            scenario = "For a one-month Sprint, the Sprint Review achieved its purpose in 90 minutes. A manager says the Guide requires it to run for the full four hours.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "A common trap: the Guide gives a maximum, not a minimum or a fixed duration.",
                2 to "The Guide has no rule for moving unused time between events; each has its own maximum.",
                3 to "Repeating an event to fill time adds waste and has no basis in the Guide.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p2-45", "psm-traps", "What is accurate?", listOf("The consultant is right: Scrum requires a release to users at the end of every Sprint", "The team should use three-month Sprints so each Sprint ends with a release", "Scrum needs a usable Increment that meets the Definition of Done every Sprint; the Guide does not require each one to be released to users", "The team can skip creating Increments until the quarterly release is near"), setOf(2),
            "The Guide makes the Scrum Team accountable for creating a valuable, useful Increment every Sprint, and says an Increment may be delivered to stakeholders before the Sprint ends. It makes no rule that every Increment must go to users at the end of each Sprint.",
            scenario = "A medical app can only be released to patients once a quarter, after an external audit. A consultant says this means the team cannot use Scrum.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Tempting because Scrum stresses delivering value often, but the Guide requires a usable Increment each Sprint, not a release to users.",
                1 to "Sprints are one month or less; three-month Sprints are not Scrum.",
                3 to "A usable, Done Increment is needed every Sprint, whatever the release schedule.",
            ),
        ),
    )
}
